WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.722
ROLLUP creates hierarchical subtotals.

00:00:02.722 --> 00:00:08.762
In this example, the hierarchy is category, then product, followed by a grand total.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:15.947
Before running the query, predict the types of rows you expect: product-level detail within each category,

00:00:15.947 --> 00:00:17.637
a category subtotal, and one final total.

00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:21.832
Run the query and inspect where grouped columns become NULL.

00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:28.867
Those NULL values may indicate that the column has been rolled up to a higher summary level.

00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:33.942
Do not interpret every NULL in a subtotal report as missing source data.

00:00:33.942 --> 00:00:38.447
In advanced grouping output, NULL can be a structural placeholder.

00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:45.007
This is why later we will use GROUPING to distinguish a subtotal placeholder from a real NULL value

00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:49.357
in the source. Advanced grouping produces several aggregation levels in one result.

00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:55.987
The challenge is interpretation: NULL may mean a subtotal placeholder rather than missing source data.

00:00:55.987 --> 00:01:02.707
Use GROUPING metadata or explicit labels so downstream users can distinguish detail groups, subtotals,

00:01:02.707 --> 00:01:03.887
and grand totals.
